What this does not do
---------------------

This permission covers aggregation, redistribution and use as a tool. It is
not a linking exception. Combining this work into a larger program so as to
form a single work still requires that larger work to be licensed under the
GNU General Public License.

Section 7 allows a downstream recipient to remove an additional permission
from a copy they convey. Removing this one leaves plain GPL version 3 or
later, which is the licence these files carry in any case.

Scope
-----

This permission is granted in respect of copyright held by the authors of the
PE32 port, which is the copyright in the code here. These programs were written
for this project. They implement the same behaviour as upstream's rather than
reproducing upstream's code, and behaviour, algorithms, file formats and the
way a project is arranged are not themselves what copyright protects.

Upstream is credited because this is a port and the design is theirs, and
because some of it does follow upstream's expression closely enough to be worth
naming. x86/M0_x86.hex2 follows the function decomposition, struct layout and
in places the instruction sequences of stage0-posix's x86/M0_x86.hex2. The hex0,
hex1 and hex2 languages are upstream's definitions. Files whose header names
only the port author -- the header stub and everything in x86/Development --
have no upstream code in them at all.

Where a file's header names upstream as well, this permission covers the port
authors' contribution to that file. If you need it to cover the whole of such a
file, ask upstream.

None of the above is legal advice, and none of it was written by a lawyer.
